{"type":"text","timestamp":1791653727087,"sessionID":"ses_a20e5b611a05a5ffc5c5","part":{"id":"prt_126e24b69001EbMKjObTT4Rzf5","sessionID":"ses_a20e5b611a05a5ffc5c5","messageID":"msg_126e23c2f0011SiG7i5QiK88Nc","type":"text","text":"I added `\"eval\"` to the forbidden list in `checks.json`, so `python3 check.py` (the check from `README.md`) now fails on any `eval(...)` call in `src`. That one line is the whole change.\n\n`check.py` already reads the code's structure, so it only flags direct calls named `eval`. Comments, strings and `obj.eval()` calls don't trip it.\n\nI tested it with temporary files, which I deleted afterwards:\n- The current repo still passes.\n- A file with `eval` only in a comment, in a string, and as `obj.eval()` passes.\n- A file with `eval(\"1+1\")` fails: the check exits with code 1 and names the file.\n\nOne gap: the check only catches calls written literally as `eval(...)`. Indirect calls like `builtins.eval(...)`, or assigning `eval` to another name, wouldn't be caught.","time":{"start":1791653727081,"end":1791653727087}}}
